// Falls: how hard a landing hurts (2026-10-20). Read by src/systems/falls.rs
// beside data/medical/wounds.ron (`Wounds::load` reads both); the food system,
// which owns the body's meters, applies a landing in its body pass
// (src/systems/food.rs, through `Wounds::take_injuries`). Disk first, with a
// copy built into the game as the fallback.
//
// IMPACT SPEED, NOT HEIGHT. Every number below is the speed, in metres a
// second, at which the floor stops a falling body, because that is what does
// the harm, and it is the same rule aboard a ship (its spin gravity), on the
// Moon and on Earth. Each comes from a fall HEIGHT a source gives, through
// v = sqrt(2 g h) with Earth's standard gravity, g = 9.80665 m/s^2:
//     0.6 m -> 3.43 m/s     2 m -> 6.26     10 ft (3.048 m) -> 7.73
//     20 ft (6.096 m) -> 10.93     30 ft -> 13.39     50 ft -> 17.29
//     60 ft -> 18.94
// The same speeds on the Moon (1.62 m/s^2) need about six times the height:
// 7.73 m/s is a 3 m drop on Earth and an 18 m one there.
//
// WHAT THE GAME LEAVES OUT. Real harm also depends on what you land on (soft
// ground, snow or water against concrete), which part of you lands first,
// your age and how you land (a trained roll spreads the blow). The game has
// one kind of landing: an adult, feet first, onto a hard floor, so the numbers
// are for that, the case the sources below mostly describe. A landing in water
// hurts nobody here, though in life a high fall into water injures and kills
// too (Warner and Demling: falls onto softer surfaces, "including water, are
// better tolerated", not harmless). Dev flight never injures, nor does the
// landing after a teleport (src/systems/falls.rs, `FallWatch`).
//
// TWO MODES, the Settings > Gameplay > Illness and injury setting
// (`illness_realistic`, systems::illness::Mode), which wounds already follow.
// Realistic reads a landing at its real speed. Forgiving, the default, reads
// it at `forgiving.speed_share` of its speed, so a fall must be faster (from
// higher) to hurt at all, and hurts less. The wound it leaves then lasts the
// share of its course every wound does in Forgiving (wounds.ron `forgiving`).
//
// Sources, each read on 2026-10-20:
//   Newgard CD, Fischer PE, Gestring M et al., "National guideline for the
//     field triage of injured patients: Recommendations of the National Expert
//     Panel on Field Triage, 2021", Journal of Trauma and Acute Care Surgery
//     93(2):e49-e60 (August 2022), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9323557/
//     (doi 10.1097/TA.0000000000003627). The criteria for taking an injured
//     person to a trauma centre include "Fall from height >10 ft (all ages)".
//     Its rationale: "The 2011 guideline specified falls >20 ft in adults and
//     >10 ft in children", and "the >10 ft criterion has good predictive
//     utility for children and adults". Its table of the 2011 guideline gives
//     "Adults: >20 ft (one story is equal to 10 ft)".
//   Warner KG, Demling RH, "The pathophysiology of free-fall injury", Annals
//     of Emergency Medicine 15(9):1088-1093 (September 1986), PubMed 3740599,
//     https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3740599/ (the abstract): "Orthopedic
//     injuries frequently are encountered in falls from two stories and less,
//     and are largely determined by the position at landing. Visceral trauma,
//     produced by deceleration forces, frequently involves the liver, spleen,
//     lung, heart and aorta, and must be suspected in falls from three stories
//     and more regardless of the landing position. Mortality from a six-story
//     fall onto a hard surface such as concrete is almost 100% for adults".
//   Risser D, Bönsch A, Schneider B, Bauer G, "Risk of dying after a free fall
//     from height", Forensic Science International 78(3):187-191 (April 1996),
//     PubMed 8635762, https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8635762/ (the abstract;
//     adults 20 to 50 landing on concrete or pavement): "The results of this
//     analysis suggest that death usually occurs when distance is more than
//     five storeys."
//   Atanasijevic TC, Savic SN, Nikolic SD, Djoki VM, "Frequency and
//     severity of injuries in correlation with the height of fall", Journal
//     of Forensic Sciences 50(3):608-612 (May 2005), PubMed 15932094,
//     https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15932094/ (the abstract; 660 fatal
//     falls): "The height of fall over 15 m appears to be a reasonable
//     boundary height beyond which the injuries of two or three body regions
//     are generally associated."
//   Lapostolle F, Gere C, Borron SW et al., "Prognostic factors in victims of
//     falls from height", Critical Care Medicine 33(6):1239-1242 (June 2005),
//     PubMed 15942337, https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15942337/ (the
//     abstract; 287 falls of more than 3 m): "Ninety-seven patients (34%)
//     ultimately died"; height of fall "median, 5.0; 3.8-8.0 vs. 2.0; 1.2-3.0
//     floors" in those who died against those who lived.
//   Knapik JJ, "United States Military Parachute Injuries: Part 2", Journal
//     of Special Operations Medicine 19(4):109-113 (2019), PubMed 31910484,
//     https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31910484/ (the abstract): injuries at the
//     Airborne School fell in 1943 with, among other changes, the
//     "elimination of dangerous and unnecessary training procedures (like
//     practice jumps from 11-foot heights)".
//   Bobbert MF, Huijing PA, van Ingen Schenau GJ, "Drop jumping. II. The
//     influence of dropping height on the biomechanics of drop jumping",
//     Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise 19(4):339-346 (August
//     1987), PubMed 3657482, https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3657482/ (the
//     abstract): healthy subjects "executed bounce drop jumps from heights of
//     20 cm ..., 40 cm ..., and 60 cm", a training exercise.
//   NHS, "Sprains and strains", page last reviewed 23 April 2024,
//     https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sprains-and-strains/: sprains are "Most
//     common in wrists, ankles, thumbs, knees"; "After 2 weeks, most sprains
//     and strains will feel better."
//   NHS, "Broken ankle", page last reviewed 5 November 2025,
//     https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/broken-ankle/: "A broken ankle usually
//     takes 6 to 8 weeks to heal, but it can take longer."
//   NHS, "Broken leg", page last reviewed 11 June 2025,
//     https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/broken-leg/: "A broken leg usually takes 6
//     to 12 weeks to heal, but it can sometimes take longer."
// (How long each wound lasts, and what it does meanwhile, is in wounds.ron and
// status_effects.csv with the NHS figures above.)
(
    // The death screen's words when a fall is what killed the player.
    cause: "a fall",
    // Said first when a landing hurts; the wound's own words follow.
    landed: "You land hard.",

    // Forgiving reads a landing at this share of its speed: 0.7 of the speed
    // is 0.49 of the height, so a fall must be about twice as high to do the
    // same, and from full health it takes about 31 m instead of 15 m to kill.
    // A GAME CHOICE, softer than the half the other modes take of a harm
    // because a share of the speed also moves every threshold up.
    forgiving: (speed_share: 0.7),

    // HEALTH A LANDING TAKES, of the body's 100, by its speed (slowest
    // first). None below the first point, straight lines between points, and
    // past the last one its line carries on. The game's Health stands for every
    // hurt at once, so these amounts are A GAME CHOICE, pinned to the sources at
    // their heights:
    harm: [
        // 2 m: a sprain's worth, little.
        (m_s: 6.26, health: 2.0),
        // 10 ft: the height past which a fall goes to a trauma centre (Newgard
        // et al.), with a broken ankle.
        (m_s: 7.73, health: 10.0),
        // 20 ft, two storeys: the top of Warner and Demling's band of mostly
        // orthopaedic injuries, and the 2011 adult line (Newgard et al.).
        (m_s: 10.93, health: 35.0),
        // 30 ft, three storeys: from here injuries inside the body "must be
        // suspected ... regardless of the landing position" (Warner and
        // Demling), so more than half a body's health.
        (m_s: 13.39, health: 60.0),
        // 50 ft, five storeys (15.2 m): "death usually occurs when distance is
        // more than five storeys" (Risser et al.), and past 15 m two or three
        // body regions are hurt together (Atanasijevic et al.). All 100: a
        // body at full health dies landing this hard, one already hurt
        // sooner. Carried on, six storeys (18.94 m/s) takes about 117, past
        // any body (Warner and Demling's "almost 100%").
        (m_s: 17.29, health: 100.0),
    ],

    // THE WOUND A LANDING LEAVES: the last step its speed reaches (one wound
    // a fall). Each is a wound row in wounds.ron and a `wound` status effect
    // whose `speed` slows walking while it heals.
    steps: [
        // 2 m. A GAME CHOICE between what lands safely and the 10 ft line: drop
        // jumps from 20 to 60 cm (3.43 m/s) are a training exercise (Bobbert
        // et al.), and the game's own jump lands at 5 m/s (a 1.27 m drop,
        // src/renderer/camera.rs JUMP_SPEED_MPS), which must never hurt. The
        // ankle, because it is where sprains are most common (NHS).
        (from_m_s: 6.26, effect: "sprained_ankle"),
        // 10 ft: the trauma-centre line (Newgard et al.); the Airborne School
        // dropped its 11-foot practice jumps as dangerous (Knapik). Below two
        // storeys the injuries are mostly broken bones, set by how you land
        // (Warner and Demling); feet first, the ankle.
        (from_m_s: 7.73, effect: "broken_ankle"),
        // 20 ft, two storeys: the 2011 adult line, the top of the orthopaedic
        // band, and the leg.
        (from_m_s: 10.93, effect: "broken_leg"),
    ],
)
